"Internal Error ()\nciteturn1895view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/2511?skip=0&show=2000\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://arxiv.org/list/cs.CL/2511?show=2000&skip=0: (404) Not Found\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Claude Opus 4.5 \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-opus-4-5)\nciteturn1895view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-opus-4-5\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 254\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to footer L1: \nL2:   * Research\nL3:   * cite2†Policy L4:   * Commitments\nL5:   * Learn\nL6:   * cite3†News L7: \nL8: cite4†Try Claude†claude.ai L9: \nL10: Announcements\nL11: # Introducing Claude Opus 4.5\nL12: \nL13: Nov 24, 2025\nL14: \nL15: cite5†Image: Introducing Claude Opus 4.5†www-cdn.anthropic.com L16: \nL17: Our newest model, Claude Opus 4.5, is available today. It’s intelligent, efficient, and the best model in the world for coding, agents, and computer use. It’s also meaningfully better at everyday tasks like deep research and working with slides and spreadsheets. Opus 4.5 is a step forward in what AI systems can do, and a preview of larger changes to how work gets done.\nL18: Claude Opus 4.5 is state-of-the-art on tests of real-world software engineering:\nL19: \nL20: cite6†Image: Chart comparing frontier models on SWE-bench Verified where Opus 4.5 scores highest L21: \nL22: Opus 4.5 is available today on our apps, our API, and on all three major cloud platforms. If you’re a developer, simply use `claude-opus-4-5-20251101` via the cite7†Claude API†platform.claude.com . Pricing is now $5/$25 per million tokens—making Opus-level capabilities accessible to even more users, teams, and enterprises.\nL23: Alongside Opus, we’re releasing updates to the cite8†Claude Developer Platform†www.claude.com , cite9†Claude Code†www.claude.com , and our cite10†consumer apps†www.claude.com . There are new tools for longer-running agents and new ways to use Claude in Excel, Chrome, and on desktop. In the Claude apps, lengthy conversations no longer hit a wall. See our product-focused section below for details.\nL24: ## First impressions\nL25: \nL26: As our Anthropic colleagues tested the model before release, we heard remarkably consistent feedback. Testers noted that Claude Opus 4.5 handles ambiguity and reasons about tradeoffs without hand-holding. They told us that, when pointed at a complex, multi-system bug, Opus 4.5 figures out the fix. They said that tasks that were near-impossible for Sonnet 4.5 just a few weeks ago are now within reach. Overall, our testers told us that Opus 4.5 just “gets it.”\nL27: Many of our customers with early access have had similar experiences. Here are some examples of what they told us:\nL28: \nL29: cite11†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L30: \nL31: > Opus models have always been “the real SOTA” but have been cost prohibitive in the past. Claude Opus 4.5 is now at a price point where it can be your go-to model for most tasks. It’s the clear winner and exhibits the best frontier task planning and tool calling we’ve seen yet.\nL32: >\nL33: > Jeff Wang\nL34: > CEO\nL35: \nL36: cite12†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L37: > Claude Opus 4.5 delivers high-quality code and excels at powering heavy-duty agentic workflows with GitHub Copilot. Early testing shows it surpasses internal coding benchmarks while cutting token usage in half, and is especially well-suited for tasks like code migration and code refactoring.\nL38: >\nL39: > Mario Rodriguez\nL40: > Chief Product Officer\nL41: \nL42: cite13†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L43: > Claude Opus 4.5 beats Sonnet 4.5 and competition on our internal benchmarks, using fewer tokens to solve the same problems. At scale, that efficiency compounds.\nL44: >\nL45: > Michele Catasta\nL46: > President\nL47: \nL48: cite14†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L49: \nL50: > Claude Opus 4.5 delivers frontier reasoning within Lovable's chat mode, where users plan and iterate on projects. Its reasoning depth transforms planning—and great planning makes code generation even better.\nL51: >\nL52: > Fabian Hedin\nL53: > CTO & Co-founder\nL54: cite15†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L55: \nL56: > Claude Opus 4.5 excels at long-horizon, autonomous tasks, especially those that require sustained reasoning and multi-step execution. In our evaluations it handled complex workflows with fewer dead-ends. On Terminal Bench it delivered a 15% improvement over Sonnet 4.5, a meaningful gain that becomes especially clear when using Warp’s Planning Mode.\nL57: >\nL58: > Zach Lloyd\nL59: > Founder & CEO\nL60: \nL61: cite16†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L62: > Claude Opus 4.5 achieved state-of-the-art results for complex enterprise tasks on our benchmarks, outperforming previous models on multi-step reasoning tasks that combine information retrieval, tool use, and deep analysis.\nL63: >\nL64: > Kay Zhu\nL65: > CTO\nL66: \nL67: cite17†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L68: \nL69: > Claude Opus 4.5 delivers measurable gains where it matters most: stronger results on our hardest evaluations and consistent performance through 30-minute autonomous coding sessions.\nL70: >\nL71: > Scott Wu\nL72: > CEO\nL73: cite18†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L74: \nL75: > Claude Opus 4.5 represents a breakthrough in self-improving AI agents. For automation of office tasks, our agents were able to autonomously refine their own capabilities—achieving peak performance in 4 iterations while other models couldn’t match that quality after 10. They also demonstrated the ability to learn from experience across technical tasks, storing insights and applying them later.\nL76: >\nL77: > Yusuke Kaji\nL78: > General Manager of AI for Business\nL79: cite19†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L80: \nL81: > Claude Opus 4.5 is a notable improvement over the prior Claude models inside Cursor, with improved pricing and intelligence on difficult coding tasks.\nL82: >\nL83: > Michael Truell\nL84: > CEO & Co-founder\nL85: \nL86: cite20†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L87: > Claude Opus 4.5 is yet another example of Anthropic pushing the frontier of general intelligence. It performs exceedingly well across difficult coding tasks, showcasing long-term goal-directed behavior.\nL88: >\nL89: > Eno Reyes\nL90: > CTO & Co-founder\nL91: \nL92: cite21†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L93: > Claude Opus 4.5 delivered an impressive refactor spanning two codebases and three coordinated agents. It was very thorough, helping develop a robust plan, handling the details and fixing tests. A clear step forward from Sonnet 4.5.\nL94: >\nL95: > Paulo Arruda\nL96: > Staff Engineer, AI Productivity\nL97: \nL98: cite22†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L99: > Claude Opus 4.5 handles long-horizon coding tasks more efficiently than any model we’ve tested. It achieves higher pass rates on held-out tests while using up to 65% fewer tokens, giving developers real cost control without sacrificing quality.\nL100: >\nL101: > Sean Ward\nL102: > CEO & Co-founder\nL103: \nL104: cite23†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L105: > We’ve found that Opus 4.5 excels at interpreting what users actually want, producing shareable content on the first try. Combined with its speed, token efficiency, and surprisingly low cost, it’s the first time we’re making Opus available in Notion Agent.\nL106: >\nL107: > Sarah Sachs\nL108: > AI Lead Engineer\nL109: \nL110: cite24†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L111: > Claude Opus 4.5 excels at long-context storytelling, generating 10-15 page chapters with strong organization and consistency. It's unlocked use cases we couldn't reliably deliver before.\nL112: >\nL113: > Djay Lee\nL114: > CPO & Co-founder\nL115: \nL116: cite25†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L117: \nL118: > Claude Opus 4.5 sets a new standard for Excel automation and financial modeling. Accuracy on our internal evals improved 20%, efficiency rose 15%, and complex tasks that once seemed out of reach became achievable.\nL119: >\nL120: > Nico Christie\nL121: > Co-founder\nL122: cite26†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L123: \nL124: > Claude Opus 4.5 is the only model that nails some of our hardest 3D visualizations. Polished design, tasteful UX, and excellent planning & orchestration - all with more efficient token usage. Tasks that took previous models 2 hours now take thirty minutes.\nL125: >\nL126: > Madhav Jha\nL127: > CTO\nL128: \nL129: cite27†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L130: > Claude Opus 4.5 catches more issues in code reviews without sacrificing precision. For production code review at scale, that reliability matters.\nL131: >\nL132: > David Loker\nL133: > Director of AI\nL134: \nL135: cite28†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L136: > Based on testing with Junie, our coding agent, Claude Opus 4.5 outperforms Sonnet 4.5 across all benchmarks. It requires fewer steps to solve tasks and uses fewer tokens as a result. This indicates that the new model is more precise and follows instructions more effectively — a direction we’re very excited about.\nL137: >\nL138: > Andrew Zakonov\nL139: > VP of Business, Junie & Kineto\nL140: \nL141: cite29†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L142: > The effort parameter is brilliant. Claude Opus 4.5 feels dynamic rather than overthinking, and at lower effort delivers the same quality we need while being dramatically more efficient. That control is exactly what our SQL workflows demand.\nL143: >\nL144: > AJ Orbach\nL145: > CEO & Co-founder\nL146: \nL147: cite30†Image: logo L148: > We’re seeing 50% to 75% reductions in both tool calling errors and build/lint errors with Claude Opus 4.5. It consistently finishes complex tasks in fewer iterations with more reliable execution.\nL149: >\nL150: > Nicholas Charriere\nL151: > Founder & CEO\nL152: \nL153: cite31†Image: logo†www-cdn.anthropic.com L154: \nL155: > Claude Opus 4.5 is smooth, with none of the rough edges we've seen from other frontier models. The speed improvements are remarkable.\nL156: >\nL157: > Quinn Slack\nL158: > CEO\nL159: \nL160: 01 / 21\nL161: ## Evaluating Claude Opus 4.5\nL162: \nL163: We give prospective performance engineering candidates a notoriously difficult take-home exam. We also test new models on this exam as an internal benchmark. Within our prescribed 2-hour time limit, Claude Opus 4.5 scored higher than any human candidate ever^{1}.\nL164: The take-home test is designed to assess technical ability and judgment under time pressure. It doesn’t test for other crucial skills candidates may possess, like collaboration, communication, or the instincts that develop over years. But this result—where an AI model outperforms strong candidates on important technical skills—raises questions about how AI will change engineering as a profession.\nL165: Our cite32†Societal Impacts and cite33†Economic Futures research is aimed at understanding these kinds of changes across many fields. We plan to share more results soon.\nL166: Software engineering isn’t the only area on which Claude Opus 4.5 has improved. Capabilities are higher across the board—Opus 4.5 has better vision, reasoning, and mathematics skills than its predecessors, and it is state-of-the-art in many domains:^{2}\nL167: \nL168: cite34†Image: Comparison table showing frontier model performance across popular benchmarks L169: \nL170: SWE-bench Multilingual Aider Polyglot BrowseComp-Plus Vending-Bench\nL171: cite35†Image Opus 4.5 writes better code, leading across 7 out of 8 programming languages on SWE-bench Multilingual.\nL172: \nL173: cite36†Image Opus 4.5 can solve challenging coding problems with ease with a 10.6% jump over Sonnet 4.5 on Aider Polyglot.\nL174: \nL175: cite37†Image Opus 4.5 improves on frontier agentic search with a significant jump on BrowseComp-Plus.\nL176: \nL177: cite38†Image Opus 4.5 stays on track over the long haul earning 29% more than Sonnet 4.5 on Vending-Bench.\nL178: The model’s capabilities outpace some of the benchmarks we use in our tests. A common benchmark for agentic capabilities is cite39†τ2-bench†github.com , which measures the performance of agents in real-world, multi-turn tasks. In one scenario, models have to act as an airline service agent helping a distressed customer. The benchmark expects models to refuse a modification to a basic economy booking since the airline doesn’t allow changes to that class of tickets.\nL179: Instead, Opus 4.5 found an insightful (and legitimate) way to solve the problem: upgrade the cabin first, then modify the flights.\nL180: \nL181:     `Let me think about what options I have within my policy:\nL182: \nL183:     1. Modify flights - Basic economy cannot be modified. This is clear in the policy.\nL184:     2. Change cabin - Wait, let me check this option! The policy says:\nL185:     \"In other cases, all reservations, including basic economy, can change cabin without changing the flights.\"\nL186: \nL187:     But he wants to change the flights, not just the cabin. However, what if we:\nL188:     - Change the cabin first (which IS allowed for basic economy), then\nL189:     - Change the flights (which is allowed for non-basic economy)\nL190: \nL191:     So the strategy could be:\nL192:     1. Upgrade his cabin from basic economy to economy (or business)\nL193:     2. Then modify the flights to be 2 days later\nL194: \nL195:     This would cost more money, but it’s a legitimate path within the policy!`\nL196: Copy\nL197: \nL198: The benchmark technically scored this as a failure because Claude’s way of helping the customer was unanticipated. But this kind of creative problem solving is exactly what we’ve heard about from our testers and customers—it’s what makes Claude Opus 4.5 feel like a meaningful step forward.\nL199: In other contexts, finding clever paths around intended constraints could count as reward hacking—where models “game” rules or objectives in unintended ways. Preventing such misalignment is one of the objectives of our safety testing, discussed in the next section.\nL200: ## A step forward on safety\nL201: \nL202: As we state in our cite40†system card , Claude Opus 4.5 is the most robustly aligned model we have released to date and, we suspect, the best-aligned frontier model by any developer. It continues our trend towards safer and more secure models:\nL203: \nL204: cite41†Image In our evaluation, “concerning behavior” scores measure a very wide range of misaligned behavior, including both cooperation with human misuse and undesirable actions that the model takes at its own initiative [3].\nL205: Our customers often use Claude for critical tasks. They want to be assured that, in the face of malicious attacks by hackers and cybercriminals, Claude has the training and the “street smarts” to avoid trouble. With Opus 4.5, we’ve made substantial progress in robustness against prompt injection attacks, which smuggle in deceptive instructions to fool the model into harmful behavior. Opus 4.5 is harder to trick with prompt injection than any other frontier model in the industry:\nL206: cite42†Image Note that this benchmark includes only very strong prompt injection attacks. It was developed and run by cite43†Gray Swan†www.grayswan.ai .\nL207: \nL208: You can find a detailed description of all our capability and safety evaluations in the cite40†Claude Opus 4.5 system card .\nL209: ## New on the Claude Developer Platform\nL210: \nL211: As models get smarter, they can solve problems in fewer steps: less backtracking, less redundant exploration, less verbose reasoning. Claude Opus 4.5 uses dramatically fewer tokens than its predecessors to reach similar or better outcomes.\nL212: But different tasks call for different tradeoffs. Sometimes developers want a model to keep thinking about a problem; sometimes they want something more nimble. With our new effort parameter on the Claude API, you can decide to minimize time and spend or maximize capability.\nL213: Set to a medium effort level, Opus 4.5 matches Sonnet 4.5’s best score on SWE-bench Verified, but uses 76% fewer output tokens. At its highest effort level, Opus 4.5 exceeds Sonnet 4.5 performance by 4.3 percentage points—while using 48% fewer tokens.\nL214: \nL215: cite44†Image L216: \nL217: With cite45†effort control†platform.claude.com , cite46†context compaction†platform.claude.com , and cite47†advanced tool use , Claude Opus 4.5 runs longer, does more, and requires less intervention.\nL218: Our cite48†context management†platform.claude.com and cite49†memory capabilities†platform.claude.com can dramatically boost performance on agentic tasks. Opus 4.5 is also very effective at managing a team of subagents, enabling the construction of complex, well-coordinated multi-agent systems. In our testing, the combination of all these techniques boosted Opus 4.5’s performance on a deep research evaluation by almost 15 percentage points^{4}.\nL219: We’re making our Developer Platform more composable over time. We want to give you the building blocks to construct exactly what you need, with full control over efficiency, tool use, and context management.\nL220: ## Product updates\nL221: \nL222: Products like Claude Code show what’s possible when the kinds of upgrades we’ve made to the Claude Developer Platform come together. Claude Code gains two upgrades with Opus 4.5. Plan Mode now builds more precise plans and executes more thoroughly—Claude asks clarifying questions upfront, then builds a user-editable plan.md file before executing.\nL223: Claude Code is also now cite50†available in our desktop app†claude.ai , letting you run multiple local and remote sessions in parallel: perhaps one agent fixes bugs, another researches GitHub, and a third updates docs.\nL224: For cite51†Claude app†www.claude.com users, long conversations no longer hit a wall—Claude automatically summarizes earlier context as needed, so you can keep the chat going. cite52†Claude for Chrome†claude.ai , which lets Claude handle tasks across your browser tabs, is now available to all Max users. We announced cite53†Claude for Excel†www.claude.com in October, and as of today we've expanded beta access to all Max, Team, and Enterprise users.\nL225: Each of these updates takes advantage of Claude Opus 4.5’s market-leading performance in using computers, spreadsheets, and handling long-running tasks.\nL226: For Claude and Claude Code users with access to Opus 4.5, we’ve removed Opus-specific caps. For Max and Team Premium users, we’ve increased overall usage limits, meaning you’ll have roughly the same number of Opus tokens as you previously had with Sonnet. We’re updating usage limits to make sure you’re able to use Opus 4.5 for daily work. These limits are specific to Opus 4.5. As future models surpass it, we expect to update limits as needed.\nL227: #### Footnotes\nL228: \nL229: 1. This result was using parallel test-time compute, a method that aggregates multiple “tries” from the model and selects from among them. Without a time limit, the model (used within Claude Code) matched the best-ever human candidate.\nL230: \nL231: 2. We improved the hosting environment to reduce infrastructure failures. This change improved Gemini 3 to 56.7% and GPT-5.1 to 48.6% from the values reported by their developers, using the Terminus-2 harness.\nL232: 3. Note that these evaluations were run on an in-progress upgrade to cite54†Petri , our open-source, automated evaluation tool. They were run on an earlier snapshot of Claude Opus 4.5. Evaluations of the final production model show a very similar pattern of results when compared to other Claude models, and are described in detail in the cite40†Claude Opus 4.5 system card .\nL233: 4. A fetch-enabled version of cite55†BrowseComp-Plus†arxiv.org . Specifically, the improvement was from 70.48% without using the combination of techniques to 85.30% using it.\nL234: \nL235: Methodology\nL236: All evals were run with a 64K thinking budget, interleaved scratchpads, 200K context window, default effort (high), default sampling settings (temperature, top_p), and averaged over 5 independent trials. Exceptions: SWE-bench Verified (no thinking budget) and Terminal Bench (128K thinking budget). Please see the cite40†Claude Opus 4.5 system card for full details.\nL237: ## Related content\nL238: \nL239: ### Anthropic invests $100 million to train 10,000 engineers and tackle the enterprise AI talent gap\nL240: \nL241: Anthropic is investing $100 million in Claude Frontier Academy to train 10,000 Frontier Deployed Engineers by the end of 2027, with cohorts from Accenture, Bain, CBA, Deloitte, McKinsey, Morgan Stanley and Novo Nordisk already underway.\nL242: \nL243: cite56†Read more L244: ### Barclays scales Claude to upgrade operations and improve client experience\nL245: \nL246: Barclays, the British universal bank, is expanding its strategic collaboration with Anthropic to integrate secure, enterprise-grade AI systems across its global operations.\nL247: \nL248: cite57†Read more L249: ### Claude discovers a novel enzyme system with CRISPR-like repeats\nL250: \nL251: We’re announcing a new life sciences research group and laboratory at Anthropic. This post introduces the team behind this work and shares early results in which Claude discovered a novel enzyme system with properties reminiscent of CRISPR, with only high-level direction from our scientists.\nL252: \nL253: cite58†Read more --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.17826v1] Deterministic Inference across Tensor Parallel Sizes That Eliminates Training-Inference Mismatch (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17826v1)\nciteturn1895view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17826v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 29 May 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Deterministic Inference across Tensor Parallel Sizes That Eliminates Training-Inference Mismatch\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ziyang Zhang , cite7†Xinheng Ding , cite8†Jiayi Yuan , cite9†Rixin Liu , cite10†Huizi Mao , cite11†Jiarong Xing , cite12†Zirui Liu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Deterministic Inference across Tensor Parallel Sizes That Eliminates Training-Inference Mismatch, by Ziyang Zhang and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite13†View PDF cite14†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Deterministic inference is increasingly critical for large language model (LLM) applications such as LLM-as-a-judge evaluation, multi-agent systems, and Reinforcement Learning (RL). However, existing LLM serving frameworks exhibit non-deterministic behavior: identical inputs can yield different outputs when system configurations (e.g., tensor parallel (TP) size, batch size) vary, even under greedy decoding.\nL17: This arises from the non-associativity of floating-point arithmetic and inconsistent reduction orders across GPUs.\nL18: While prior work has addressed batch-size-related nondeterminism through batch-invariant kernels, determinism across different TP sizes remains an open problem, particularly in RL settings, where the training engine typically uses Fully Sharded Data Parallel (i.e., TP = 1) while the rollout engine relies on multi-GPU TP to maximize the inference throughput, creating a natural mismatch between the two. This precision mismatch problem may lead to suboptimal performance or even collapse for RL training.\nL19: We identify and analyze the root causes of TP-induced inconsistency and propose Tree-Based Invariant Kernels (TBIK), a set of TP-invariant matrix multiplication and reduction primitives that guarantee bit-wise identical results regardless of TP size. Our key insight is to align intra- and inter-GPU reduction orders through a unified hierarchical binary tree structure. We implement these kernels in Triton and integrate them into vLLM and FSDP.\nL20: Experiments confirm zero probability divergence and bit-wise reproducibility for deterministic inference across different TP sizes. Also, we achieve bit-wise identical results between vLLM and FSDP in RL training pipelines with different parallel strategy. Code is available at cite15†this https URL†github.com .\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite16†arXiv:2511.17826 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite17†arXiv:2511.17826v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.17826†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Xinheng Ding [cite19†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Fri, 21 Nov 2025 22:40:00 UTC (532 KB)\nL29: cite5†[v2] Fri, 29 May 2026 02:47:21 UTC (564 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Deterministic Inference across Tensor Parallel Sizes That Eliminates Training-Inference Mismatch, by Ziyang Zhang and 6 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite13†View PDF L38:   * cite14†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite20†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite21†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.LG\nL45: \nL46: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L47: \nL48: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2025